/* The floor: judgement, which no bank of recall questions can reach.

   Every option is something a competent person might actually do. There are no
   straw men here, because the whole lesson is the distance between the best
   answer and the second best one, and a scenario with an obviously stupid
   option teaches nothing but how to spot an obviously stupid option.

   Each entry was written by one pass and then handed to a second told to
   REFUTE it: to argue the marked answer was not the best of the four and that
   the reasoning was wrong about wine. Fifty three of the fifty four items in
   this file and its pairing sibling came back with a correction, and the
   corrections are in. That hit rate is not a failure of the first pass; it is
   what happens when the second pass is told to attack rather than approve.

   FIELDS
     id    stable, f- prefixed; ST.floor is keyed by it, so never renumber
     lvl   which rank this is pitched at
     s     the situation
     opts  four actions
     best  the index of the one a master takes
     why   why that one, and specifically why the nearest wrong one is wrong */

const FLOOR = [
  { id: "f-corked-fourtop", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A four top orders a bottle of Chablis Premier Cru. You pour the host a taste, he nods and says it is lovely. Your own check pour reads damp cardboard and a short, muted finish: light taint, but taint. The other three are waiting with their glasses forward.",
    opts: ["Pour the table. The host tasted it and approved it.", "Ask the host to smell it again and tell you what he picks up.", "Take the bottle away, tell the table the bottle is not right, and return with a fresh one and a fresh taste for the host.", "Pour the table, then open a second bottle at the back and compare before deciding whether to swap."],
    best: 2,
    why: "The host's taste confirms the wine is the one he ordered. Judging its condition is your job, and low level taint is exactly the fault a guest at dinner will miss and still be dulled by all night. The nearest miss is asking him to smell again: it makes his nose the arbiter, invites him to defend a position he has taken in front of three friends, and costs you the minute you could spend fetching the replacement." },
  { id: "f-crumbling-cork", lvl: "intro",
    s: "A 1998 Barolo, standing since yesterday. The worm goes in clean but the cork gives way at half depth and a shower of crumb sits on the wine. Roughly half the cork is still in the neck and four guests are watching your hands.",
    opts: ["Push the rest of the cork through into the bottle, then decant through a fine strainer.", "Switch to the two prong, work the remaining cork out against the glass, and decant off any crumb.", "Drive the worm deeper into what is left and pull again.", "Take the bottle to the back, sort it out of sight, and return with it decanted."],
    best: 1,
    why: "The two prong is right because it grips the remnant from the sides and never asks a torn cork to hold a thread. Lead with the long blade down the side where the cork sits proudest, work the short blade in opposite it, then rock and twist as you lift so the cork turns out rather than tears. Tell the table plainly that the cork has come apart and the wine is sound, then decant through muslin or a fine mesh funnel, since crumb is already floating and a 1998 Barolo wants decanting in any case. Be accurate about the tool: the two prong is built for fragile corks that are still whole, it is harder work on one a worm has already breached, and the tool actually made for this bottle is a Durand, worm and two prong together. Pushing the plug through is the nearest miss: it displaces wine, lifts the sediment you settled overnight back into suspension, and leaves a bobbing cork fouling every pour, and the chipping risk is the inside of the neck and lip, not the shoulder. Driving the worm in again shreds what is left. Working out of sight costs the guest the cork, the label and the ceremony, and invites the thought that the bottle was swapped." },
  { id: "f-reduction-cornas", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "A guest orders a young Cornas. It opens tight and struck: matchstick, a little drain, almost no fruit behind it. He pushes the glass back and says it smells of eggs and is clearly off. He is right about what he smells and wrong about what it is.",
    opts: ["Replace it without discussion. The guest decides.", "Tell him the wine is reduced, take it away and decant it hard, then taste it with him in a few minutes with the offer to change it still standing.", "Explain that this is the producer's style and ask him to give the wine twenty minutes.", "Take the bottle to the pass, taste it out of his sight, and decide from there."],
    best: 1,
    why: "Eggs is hydrogen sulphide, and with struck match and a drain note on a young Cornas that is reduction, not a faulty bottle. Reduction is the one suspected fault you can usually clear in front of the guest: a hard splash decant drives off the volatile sulphides. So name it, take the bottle, decant it properly, and come back and taste it with him. Replacing on demand loses not because the guest should not decide but because the next bottle of the same young Cornas will smell exactly the same: you have cost the house a bottle and solved nothing, and if he still wants out you change the wine, not the bottle. Option 3 is wrong only in where it happens. Tasting the wine yourself is correct and you should, but doing it at the pass and returning with a verdict takes him out of his own decision. Option 2 wins the argument and hands him back a glass he has already refused. One caveat belongs in the answer: the heavier, mercaptan end of reduction does not always blow off, and when the decant fails to clear it you pour him something else without further discussion." },
  { id: "f-va-over-line", lvl: "master",
    s: "A regular orders SP68 from Arianna Occhipinti off your natural section. The bottle comes up lifted: acetone sitting over the frappato fruit, and on the palate the finish dries and shortens rather than lengthening. To you it is past lift and into solvent. The guest has said nothing and looks perfectly happy.",
    opts: ["Serve it. Volatility belongs to the style and the guest chose the section.", "Pour it, say nothing, and watch whether the table drinks it.", "Tell the guest this bottle is showing more volatility than it should, open a second of the same wine, and compare before you do anything else.", "Pull it, replace it with a wine you know will please, and absorb the cost."],
    best: 2,
    why: "Open the second bottle, but open it before you speak, not after. Taste it yourself and with your captain away from the table. If it matches, the lift is the cuvee as it sits in your cellar, the guest who is enjoying his glass keeps it, and the decision you now own is what to do with the rest of the case and whether the wine stays on the list. If the second is clean, carry it to the table, change the bottle, and say only that you preferred the second. Speaking first spends the guest's confidence on evidence you do not yet have. On the wine itself: ethyl acetate and acetic acid are made by spoilage organisms, in the winery or in bottle, so this is not cork taint. A poor seal can feed them by admitting oxygen, but the diagnosis in front of you is bottle variation against lot character. SP68 rosso is frappato with nero d'Avola, and Occhipinti's wines carry real volatility by design, so the line you are judging is one of degree and your standard is whether the finish lengthens or dries. Here it dries, and that is the reason to investigate. The swap in option 3 remains the nearest miss for exactly the reason stated." },
  { id: "f-heat-struck-sancerre", lvl: "certified",
    s: "You pull a two year old Sancerre from the back of the shelf by the pass. The capsule sits proud of the lip, there is a sticky line of dried wine under the foil, and the colour through the glass is a deep gold. The table ordered it five minutes ago and their first course is up.",
    opts: ["Present it and open it at the table as normal. Let the host taste and decide.", "Open it away from the table, confirm, and go back with the news and a comparable bottle already in your hand.", "Carry it to the table, show them the pushed cork, and explain the risk before you open it.", "Set it aside unopened for a supplier claim and offer the table something else."],
    best: 1,
    why: "You do not present a bottle you have already condemned, and you do not ask a guest to rule on your own cellar. A pushed capsule, a sticky seepage line under the foil and deep gold in a two year old Sancerre are heat damage, and that diagnosis is made in the service station, not at the table. Open it in the back anyway, because it costs thirty seconds and it tells you how far gone the wine is before you speak to the guest and what to do with every other bottle on that shelf tonight. Do not open it for a supplier claim: a bottle held two years by the pass is the house's own fault and no distributor owes you credit for it, so the money lost here is the price of learning that spot is too warm to store wine. Then go back with a comparable bottle already in hand and a short apology, because the guest needs something to say yes to, not a problem to solve while their food dies. Tell the kitchen to hold the course on your way. Option 3 keeps the wine off the table but sends you back empty handed to negotiate, and the claim it is built on does not exist. Option 2 is honest and still wrong: it hands the guest your failure and asks them to adjudicate it. Option 0 is the only one that could put a cooked wine in a glass, and it is indefensible." },
  { id: "f-sound-but-unloved", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A couple order a bottle of Muscadet Sevre et Maine sur lie expecting something rich. It is lean, saline and entirely correct. Two sips in she says she does not like it, it tastes sour to her. He shrugs and says he is easy either way. Their oysters are thirty seconds from the table.",
    opts: ["Take it back, replace it with a wine that matches what she describes, and find the open bottle a home.", "Explain that the wine is sound and cannot be changed, and offer her a glass of something else alongside it.", "Ask her to try it with the oysters landing now before she decides.", "Leave the bottle for him and bring her a glass of something she will like."],
    best: 0,
    why: "The guest's palate is the only verdict that counts at the table. The bottle goes back and she gets something with more weight: a Chablis, a Mâcon-Villages, an Alsace Pinot Blanc. The house is not absorbing a write-off. Muscadet Sevre et Maine sur lie is the easiest bottle in the building to move beside a raw bar, so it transfers to the by-the-glass list and sells out before service ends. Do not plan on the staff line as a disposal route, and do not price the decision as a lost bottle. Option [2] is the nearest miss, but not for the reason usually given. The pairing is correct and the oysters land in half a minute, so the wine would have answered her. The fault is procedural: you never ask a guest to re-argue a verdict she has already given. Do both at once. Apologise, leave the bottle standing while you fetch the replacement, and let the oysters land in the gap. If she reaches for the glass again on her own, she has changed her own mind and the bottle stays sold." },
  { id: "f-tired-fino", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "A runner brings back a glass of en rama fino from table nine: the guest says it is oxidised. Ten minutes earlier the bar sent one back for the same reason. The bottle has been open in the fridge since Tuesday and it is Sunday. Against one you open now, the old bottle is flat, bitter and browning at the edge.",
    opts: ["Tell the team the style is meant to smell like that and send the glass back out.", "Replace the guest's glass from the same bottle and move on.", "Bin the open bottle, replace both glasses from the fresh one, and set a rule that an open fino lives three days and then goes to the kitchen.", "Take fino off the by the glass list. It keeps coming back and it is costing you bottles."],
    best: 2,
    why: "Two glasses back in ten minutes, and a side by side against a fresh bottle settles it: the wine is tired, not typical. Fino is aged biologically under flor, which shields it from oxygen, so a browning, bitter, flat glass is never the house style: en rama is bottled with little or no filtration and fades faster than standard fino, not slower. Re-pour both guests from the fresh bottle and send the remainder of the old one to the kitchen rather than the bin. The control is a dated tag at open, a three day life on the fridge, and a taste before every service, because a half empty bottle in a busy fridge can fail sooner than the calendar says. Pulling fino from the list punishes the category for a storage failure that half bottles and thirty seconds of tasting will fix." },
  { id: "f-curtain-at-forty", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A four-top sits at 7.05 and tells you they are at the theatre for 7.45. They order two starters, two mains to share and a bottle of the 2020 Chablis Premier Cru Montmains from William Fevre. The kitchen is running a twenty minute ticket and nobody has told them about the curtain.",
    opts: ["Open and pour the Chablis straight away, then walk the curtain time down to the kitchen.", "Walk the curtain time to the kitchen first and get the ticket re-fired, then open and pour.", "Suggest two glasses each instead of the bottle so nothing is left behind.", "Take the order as written and ask the table to signal when they need the bill."],
    best: 1,
    why: "The kitchen holds the only clock you cannot reset, so it gets the information first. Flag the table as pre-theatre at the pass and the chef can drop both starters now and hold the mains on a short fire, which is the difference between plates landing at 7.20 and plates landing at 7.40. Pouring first does not cost you the walk, it costs the whole table beat: label, taste, four pours, and a question you cannot refuse, two to three minutes with your back to the pass. Put the bottle in ice on your way past, walk the ticket, then come back, open it properly and tell the table what time their mains will land. Suggesting glasses instead is not just paternalistic, it is unworkable: a William Fevre Montmains premier cru is not a by-the-glass pour in most houses, so you either open the bottle anyway or downgrade the table and shrink the cheque for nothing." },
  { id: "f-marked-glassware", lvl: "intro",
    s: "A runner sets six glasses on a four-top where two guests are waiting on a decanted bottle. Under the lamp the bowls are water spotted and one carries a lip print from the wash. The decanter is on your tray and the guests are watching it.",
    opts: ["Polish them at the table with your service cloth, quickly, then pour.", "Swap the marked ones and leave the rest.", "Lift all six, replace them with polished glasses, then pour, with no explanation.", "Pour now so the wine is not held, and change the glasses on the next pass."],
    best: 2,
    why: "Fresh glass before wine, every time. You lift the whole set, because a spotted bowl and a lip print come out of one rack and the stems you cannot fault under the lamp came from the same rack. Do it in one trip with a tray of polished glasses, and reset to the cover the table actually needs rather than to six because six were there. Silence is not the standard, discretion is: a level line as you set them down, \"fresh glasses for you\", costs nothing and reads as intent, where clearing six stems in front of two watching guests without a word reads as a problem you will not name. Skip the apology, which does invite the hunt. Swapping only the marked glasses is the nearest wrong answer, because it leaves two standards on one table. Polishing at the table finishes a wash in the guests' eyeline with your cloth and your hands. Pouring first puts an unsanitised glass in a guest's hand, and a decanted wine loses nothing sitting on your tray for ninety seconds, so the urgency in that option is invented. The rest of the correction happens behind you: the rack goes back to the dish pit and the polish station is pulled before the next table is set." },
  { id: "f-cork-on-the-move", lvl: "certified",
    s: "You are opening a bottle of Bollinger Special Cuvee at the table. The cage is off, your thumb is over the cork, and the bottle is warmer than it should be. The cork lifts hard against your hand and starts to travel on its own. Two guests are leaning in to watch.",
    opts: ["Let it come and catch it in the napkin so nothing is spilled.", "Keep the thumb down, angle the bottle away from everyone, and ease the cork out against your grip, taking the pressure through your hand.", "Press the cork back down a few millimetres to re-seat it, then start again.", "Set the bottle on the station, refit the cage, and let it rest before trying again."],
    best: 1,
    why: "The cage is off and the cork is already moving, so the only thing left to decide is where it goes. Keep the thumb loaded, turn your shoulder between the bottle and the two guests, point the neck at nobody and nothing breakable, and let the cork out slowly against your grip so the gas sighs instead of firing. The bottle is warm, so expect it to push and expect foam the moment the seal breaks: keep the neck angled, keep your hand on the cork until it is fully clear, and have a glass within reach. The real trap here is the napkin catch, because it looks gracious and saves the linen while surrendering the only thing that matters, which is direction. Re-seating is not a temptation, it is impossible: the head will not pass back into the neck. Carrying a live bottle to the station and refitting the cage puts two hands and your face around a moving cork and does nothing about the pressure." },
  { id: "f-last-bottle-gone", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A table of six orders the 2018 Barbaresco from Produttori del Barbaresco. You go down and the slot is empty: the last bottle went at lunch and nobody pulled the tag. The guest chose it himself and read the producer aloud to the table.",
    opts: ["Go back, apologise, and ask what else they would like.", "Go back, apologise, name two wines you would pour instead at or under the same price, and lead with the one you would drink.", "Bring a more expensive Barbaresco and charge the listed price of the 2018.", "Bring the 2019 of the same wine and mention the vintage when you show the label."],
    best: 1,
    why: "You made the gap, so you close it, and you close it fast: the table has been waiting since he said the name out loud. Go back yourself, say the bottle is gone and that it is ours to answer for, and arrive with the answer already made. Two wines, one line each, and say which one you would drink. The first of the two should carry the same name if the cellar holds it. Produttori del Barbaresco bottles a straight Barbaresco every vintage and single vineyard Riserve in the years it declares them, so check the bins before you climb the stairs: a man who bought that label in front of five people takes another vintage of it far more happily than a stranger at the same money. If there is nothing of theirs left, go to another serious house in Barbaresco or Barolo at or under his price and say in one sentence why. Asking what else they would like is the nearest wrong answer, because it hands your problem back to a guest who already chose once. Sending up the 2019 and mentioning the vintage at the label is a substitution he never agreed to, disclosed in public at his own table. Comping the upgrade spends the house's money on a problem a sentence would have solved and teaches him a price he will expect again. Then fix the cause before service ends: 86 the wine in the POS, pull the tag, and tell the floor." },
  { id: "f-cloudy-carry-in", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "A regular brings his own bottle for corkage, a 1985 Barolo from Bruno Giacosa, carried across town in a tote bag. You hold it to the light at the station and it is cloudy from neck to punt. He has just sat down, it is an anniversary, and they have booked the whole evening.",
    opts: ["Decant it now over a candle, going slowly and leaving more than usual in the bottle.", "Stand it upright in the cellar, tell him plainly that it needs an hour, and put a glass of something in front of them while it settles.", "Double decant it now through a fine funnel screen so the deposit is caught.", "Serve it from a cradle without decanting so the deposit is never lifted."],
    best: 1,
    why: "A bottle carried across town has its deposit in suspension, and suspended sediment cannot be read against a candle. There is no line to watch, so you pour lees into the decanter with flawless technique. Standing it upright is the only move aimed at the actual problem, and it buys a second thing worth as much: the bottle arrived warm and the hour brings it back to cellar temperature. Be honest about what the hour does. It settles the heavy deposit, not every trace, so you decant off the top when it is ready and leave a generous heel. Promise that and you keep it. Promise clarity and you do not. A guest with the whole evening booked takes that trade if you ask him properly, and the glass you put in front of the table is hospitality and a sale in the same gesture. The candle decant is the nearest miss: the right tool, used on a wine that is not ready for it. The funnel screen catches grit and passes fine haze, and pouring a 1985 Barolo back into its own bottle churns a fragile wine twice for nothing. The cradle simply leaves the deposit in the bottle until the second pour lifts it again." },
  { id: "f-wrong-vintage-up", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "The guest ordered the 2015 Chablis Grand Cru Les Clos from Vincent Dauvissat, at the price he read off the page. The runner brings up the 2019. He is a regular who orders by vintage, and the table of six is deep in conversation.",
    opts: ["Present the 2019 at the table, name the vintage as you show the label, and pour if he nods.", "Hold the bottle at the station, check the cellar yourself, and if the 2015 is gone go back with the fact, the price of the 2019, and one alternative.", "Serve the 2019 at the 2015 price and leave the difference unmentioned.", "Take the 2019 away and bring a different Chablis of the same standing."],
    best: 1,
    why: "Two things are unsettled: whether the 2015 is still in the building, and what the guest is now being charged. Neither is settled at the table, so a word to the host on the way past is all that goes there: a moment on the vintage, then you check the cellar yourself, because the runner has already been wrong once. Presenting the 2019 and naming the vintage is the nearest wrong answer. It is honest, but it hands a decision to a man sitting with five other people, and he will take the bottle to end the interruption. Pouring the 2019 at the 2015 price fails twice over: you are serving a wine that was not ordered and billing it as one that was, which is misrepresentation in any jurisdiction that inspects a wine list, and you are writing off the difference between two vintages of Dauvissat Les Clos on your own authority. Swapping in another Chablis takes the choice away from a guest whose whole order was a producer and a year. Come back with the fact, the 2019's price as the list carries it, and one alternative you can stand behind, and let him decide in ten seconds." },
  { id: "f-fragile-seventy-eight", lvl: "master",
    s: "A 1978 Clos de la Roche from Domaine Dujac, ordered by a guest who knows exactly what he has. The fill is into the shoulder, the capsule is stained and the cork looks tired. The bottle has stood since morning and the deposit has settled. Four covers, relaxed, no pressure on the table at all.",
    opts: ["Decant it an hour before service so it has time to open.", "Draw the cork at the table, pour yourself a small taste, and decide from the glass whether it comes off the deposit or stays in the bottle.", "Decant it slowly into a narrow decanter at the table and serve immediately.", "Leave it in the bottle, pour carefully from standing, and stop at the deposit."],
    best: 1,
    why: "Open it at the table with a two-prong, because a forty-eight-year-old cork that already looks soft will shred on a worm, and pour yourself a short taste from the standing bottle without rousing the deposit. The outside of the bottle does tell you something: the ullage, the stained capsule and the tired cork say this wine has lived a long time and may be past its best. What it cannot tell you is whether the wine is frail or merely closed, and that decides everything. Taste, then act. Reduced or tight, decant slowly into a narrow decanter at the table and serve at once. Delicate and fading, leave it standing and pour off the deposit. Option 2 commits the whole bottle to air before you know a thing, option 0 serves the guest the memory of the wine rather than the wine, and option 3 forfeits the decant you will need anyway if that cork crumbles into the bottle. Four relaxed covers and a guest who knows exactly what he ordered: tell him what you found in the glass and give him the call. It costs half a minute and it is the part of the evening he is actually paying you for." },
  { id: "f-host-orders-barolo", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "A table of six, a host who is clearly paying and clearly wants to be seen choosing well. Before anyone has spoken to the kitchen he orders two bottles of 2016 Barolo for the table. Ten minutes later the orders come in: two sole, one salad, three beef. The Barolo is already decanted and breathing on your station.",
    opts: ["Pour the Barolo to all six as ordered. He chose for his table and that is his right.", "Step to the host away from the table and ask whether he would like a white on the table alongside the Barolo for the fish courses, offering to start it by the glass.", "Tell the host at the table that Barolo will overwhelm the sole and suggest he swap one bottle for a white.", "Go quietly to the two fish guests and offer each a glass of white with your compliments."],
    best: 1,
    why: "Two bottles are open and decanted, so nothing goes back. The only question left is what else reaches the table, and the host is the one who decides it. Take it to him off the table, quietly, and frame it as an addition: a white by the glass to start for the fish courses, with the Barolo carrying the beef. He hears an offer of generosity rather than a correction of his order, and he keeps the credit for looking after his guests. Barolo at ten years old still carries firm nebbiolo tannin and high acid, which strips lean sole and turns metallic against it, so the two fish guests are the real problem to solve. Telling him this at the table solves it by spending his standing in front of five people, and he will remember that longer than he remembers the fish. Going straight to the fish guests solves it by telling them, silently, that their host did not think of them. Pouring all six as ordered is defensible and costs the house nothing, but it leaves two glasses untouched and a sixth guest on salad with the same problem. The discreet approach protects the host, keeps both bottles sold, and adds glass revenue on top." },
  { id: "f-quiet-word-not-drinking", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A six-top on the tasting menu, wine pairings agreed for the whole table by the host. Clearing the second course you lean in to a woman on the banquette and she says, low, that she will not be drinking tonight, she is pregnant, and please do not make anything of it. The third pairing is already poured on your pass.",
    opts: ["Pour her the pairings as normal so nothing at her place looks different, and let her leave them untouched.", "Pour her a token measure of each pairing, enough to look right, and say nothing to anyone.", "Build a non-alcoholic pairing for her, serve it in the same stemware at the same moment as every other glass, and settle the difference on the bill without itemising it at the table.", "Have a discreet word with the host and ask whether he would like a non-alcoholic pairing for one guest."],
    best: 2,
    why: "She told one person and asked that it travel no further, so the whole answer is symmetry and silence: a non-alcoholic pairing in the same stemware, landing with everyone else's glass, nothing announced and nothing to explain. Taking it to the host hands him the one fact she chose to keep, and by the cheese course the table knows. Pouring her wine she has refused is worse, and leaving a row of full untouched glasses at her place is its own announcement. Two things the answer has to get right on a real floor. The glass already on the pass cannot be replaced in time, so put water in the correct stem now and have the non-alcoholic run ready from the next course. And confirm in the same low voice that she wants a pairing at all rather than simply water, because deciding for her is a form of attention too. On the money, price the non-alcoholic pairing at the wine pairing price and absorb any difference in the house, so the bill the host reads shows six pairings and tells him nothing." },
  { id: "f-fleurie-is-gamay", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "You pour a Fleurie for a four-top. The man who ordered it swirls, tastes, and tells his party that this is Burgundy, so it is Pinot Noir, the lighter and cleverer end of it. He is right about the lift and the red fruit he describes. He is wrong about the grape, and his three guests are nodding.",
    opts: ["Correct him cleanly and briefly at the table: Fleurie is Beaujolais and the grape is Gamay.", "Say nothing and carry on with service. The wine is sound and no one is harmed.", "Agree with the part he got right, then set the fact beside it for the whole table: that lift he is describing is Gamay, from Fleurie in the Beaujolais crus.", "Wait until he steps outside or to the cloakroom, then tell him privately."],
    best: 2,
    why: "Correct the grape and leave the host his standing. His tasting note was accurate: Fleurie is lifted and red-fruited, and the grape behind that is Gamay, from one of the ten Beaujolais crus. Laid beside what he already said, the fact arrives as something added, not as a correction he has to absorb in front of guests he brought out. Say nothing and those three repeat the error for years, which is the one outcome a floor cannot accept. Correcting him flatly at the table lands the same fact and charges him for it publicly. Waiting for the cloakroom protects him and leaves the table misinformed. Note what does not need correcting: Beaujolais sits inside the greater Burgundy region and its crus may be declassified to Bourgogne rouge, so his word Burgundy is fair. The grape is the only thing to put right." },
  { id: "f-birthday-sixty-pounds", lvl: "intro",
    s: "A four-top sits down for a birthday. While the others are hanging coats the man who booked catches you and says quietly that it is her birthday, they want something special, and they are at about sixty pounds for the wine. Your list opens at forty-five and the Champagne by the glass is eighteen.",
    opts: ["Hand him the list and leave him to it so he can decide in his own time.", "Come back to him, point at two bottles at or under sixty that you would happily drink yourself, and offer to send a candle out with dessert.", "Recommend the sixty-five pound bottle. It is far better value and only a little over what he said.", "Suggest four glasses of Champagne instead of a bottle so the table gets a proper toast."],
    best: 1,
    why: "He gave you a number, so the number is the brief. Two named bottles at or under sixty is the work he actually asked for, and it is done quietly away from the table so his wife never hears a price on her own birthday. The candle costs the house nothing and is the part of the evening she will remember. Recommending the sixty-five pound bottle is the common failure: five pounds is small to you, and it forces him to choose between his budget and looking careful in front of his wife. Four glasses of Champagne comes to seventy-two pounds for half a litre of wine, over his number and less in the glass than the bottle he could have had. Handing him the list returns the work to a man who has already asked for help." },
  { id: "f-regulars-same-bottle", lvl: "certified",
    s: "Thursday, seven o'clock, the same two-top by the window. He has ordered the same Chianti Classico every week for six years. You have just taken delivery of a Carmignano you think he would love, and the Chianti has changed hands at the estate with the new vintage.",
    opts: ["Bring his Chianti as always and say nothing. That bottle is why he comes.", "Bring his Chianti as always, and set down beside it a small pour of the Carmignano for him to try, no charge and no obligation.", "Tell him the new vintage has changed in style and steer him towards something you rate more highly this year.", "Ask him at the table whether he fancies trying something different tonight."],
    best: 1,
    why: "Bring the bottle he came for. You do not renegotiate a six-year ritual at the door, and you do not put doubt on the one wine he trusts you for. But the estate changed hands with this vintage and he will taste that, so say it quietly as you pour: one sentence, no verdict, so that if the wine reads differently tonight he knows why and he heard it from you first rather than working it out alone. Then set the Carmignano down beside it, a small pour, no charge, no obligation. Carmignano is Sangiovese with ten to twenty percent Cabernet, the same spine he has drunk for six years with a little more flesh, so it reads as a neighbour to his wine rather than a replacement for it, and the cost of the pour is the cheapest way you will ever open a new listing. Option 0 hides a material change to the one bottle he buys from you, and you lose him the week he notices. Option 2 marks his wine down for him and sells over the top of a man who did not ask. Option 3 makes him answer at the table, with company present, a question he settled six years ago." },
  { id: "f-you-choose-no-brief", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A two-top, steak and halibut ordered. The woman closes the wine list, hands it back to you and says you choose, she trusts you. No grape, no region, no number, and her guest is watching to see how she is looked after.",
    opts: ["Ask her directly what she would like to spend so that you can choose accurately.", "Open the list in front of her, put a finger on one bottle and say this is where you would go, letting her agree or move you up or down without either of you saying a number aloud.", "Choose a bottle in the middle of the list that you believe in and bring it. She asked you to decide.", "Ask what she has drunk and enjoyed recently, and build the recommendation from her answer."],
    best: 1,
    why: "She has given you everything except the one fact you cannot guess. Style, weight and the split between steak and halibut you can read from the table and the order. Price you cannot, and she will not say a number in front of her guest, because the whole point of handing the list back was to look unbothered by it. So you take the number out of the conversation. Open the list where she can see it, rest a finger on one bottle you would genuinely pour, name the wine out loud and say that this is where you would go. The guest hears a wine. She sees a price. She nods, or she moves your finger, and nobody has said a figure.\n\nPoint at one bottle, not a range, and point a little below what you suspect she will take, so that moving up is a pleasure and staying put is not a retreat. Then choose and go. She said you choose, so do not follow the silent price check with a questionnaire.\n\nAsking what she would like to spend is honest and it is also the thing she was avoiding. It hands her the bill in front of her guest. Asking what she has drunk recently is good technique and the right second move once you know the ceiling, since it buys style, not the number, and it is the number that sours the end of the evening. Guessing at the middle of the list is a gamble with the house's money in both directions: undershoot and you have under-served a guest who was ready to spend, overshoot and you have ambushed her at the end of dinner. For the bottle itself, one mid-weight red bridges both plates better than two glasses of compromise." },
  { id: "f-third-bottle-had-enough", lvl: "master",
    s: "A four-top, three bottles down, half past ten. One man has gone loud, his consonants are going, and he has twice knocked the base of his glass on the table. His three companions have stopped talking and are looking at the cloth. He raises a hand and asks you for another bottle, naming one of the more expensive Burgundies on the list.",
    opts: ["Bring the bottle, open it slowly, pour half measures and keep water and bread going until the table finishes on its own.", "Tell him politely at the table that you are not able to serve more alcohol tonight, and offer water and coffee.", "Step away, tell your manager, then say a quiet word to the soberest guest at the table: no more wine tonight, dessert and coffee are on you, and let that guest carry it to him.", "Tell him the Burgundy he wants is finished and offer a lighter, lower alcohol bottle in its place."],
    best: 2,
    why: "He is visibly intoxicated, so service stops. That is the licence, not a judgement call, and it is not negotiable by how good the table's spend has been. The judgement is who says it and in front of whom. Step away and tell your manager first, so the refusal is the house's decision rather than one server's, and so the bar knows not to pour if he presents himself there. Then take it quietly to the soberest person at the table, with dessert and coffee on us. His friend can carry the news without turning him into a spectacle in front of the people he came with, and the table keeps what is left of its evening. Two cautions on running it. You still own the refusal: if he asks you directly again, answer him yourself, warmly and without negotiating, and do not leave his friend to fight it alone. And be ready for the party to settle up and go rather than stay for pudding: the lost fourth bottle is cheaper than the licence or the room. Note that book and floor agree here. Pouring half measures behind bread and water is serving him and calling it management. Refusing him out loud at the table is honest but public, and the public part is what turns a manageable night into a scene. Telling him the Burgundy has run out is a lie that still puts alcohol in him, and it dies the moment another table is seen with that bottle." },
  { id: "f-corkage-call", lvl: "intro",
    s: "You pick up the phone on a Tuesday afternoon. A guest booking a table for four on Saturday for an anniversary asks whether they can bring a bottle of their own. The house policy, printed on the website, is thirty pounds corkage, one bottle per two guests, and nothing that already appears on the list. You have been on the floor three weeks.",
    opts: ["Quote the fee, the one bottle per two guests limit and the no duplicates rule, take the wine's name and vintage, and put it on the booking note", "Quote the thirty pounds, which is what they asked about, and sort out the rest when they arrive on the night", "Tell them you will check with the head sommelier and ring them back within the hour", "Waive the corkage because it is an anniversary and you want the booking confirmed"],
    best: 0,
    why: "The policy is published, so the newest person on the floor is expected to state all three parts of it without asking anyone, and stating it on Tuesday is what stops the argument on Saturday. Taking the name and vintage is only worth doing if it closes the loop: check the bottle against the list that afternoon and ring the guest back if it is already on there. A booking note nobody acts on hands the guest the same surprise at the table that quoting only the fee does. Ringing the head sommelier back within the hour is over-escalation on information already on the website, and it teaches a new hire to distrust the printed policy. The answer that does real damage is waiving the corkage: that is the house's money, a three-week hire has no authority to give it away, and the guest will expect the same waiver next visit and say so to the next table." },
  { id: "f-last-glass-sancerre", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A bottle of Sancerre was opened for the by the glass list two evenings ago and there is one glass left in it. You taste it: nothing faulty, but the fruit has gone flat and the finish is short. A four top orders one glass of it alongside three other drinks. There is one fresh bottle left on the shelf.",
    opts: ["Tip the remainder away, open the fresh bottle, and pour the glass from that", "Pour the last glass: it is dull but it is sound, and it is the end of an open bottle either way", "Pour it and tell the guest it is the end of the bottle so they can send it back if they would rather", "Tell the table the Sancerre has run out and offer them a different white by the glass"],
    best: 0,
    why: "You are tipping one glass, not a bottle, and that is the entire cost of the right answer. Weigh one pour of Sancerre against a four top who quietly decide your white wines are tired and never tell you. Open the fresh bottle and pour from that. The nearest wrong answer is the honest warning before you pour. It sounds like transparency, but it hands the guest your job, and almost nobody sends back a glass the sommelier has just set down in front of them. Two things follow that the table never sees. The fresh bottle is your last, so tell the floor that Sancerre by the glass is now finite before someone promises four glasses of it to a six top, and know what you are switching people to when it goes. And the tired remainder goes to the kitchen, not the sink: it is sound wine and it will make a beurre blanc." },
  { id: "f-cloudy-bay-swap", lvl: "certified",
    s: "A table of four, one of them clearly hosting. He scans the list for about eight seconds, points at Cloudy Bay Sauvignon Blanc at sixty eight pounds and says they will have two of those. You have Dog Point Sauvignon Blanc at forty eight, made by the viticulturist and the winemaker who ran Cloudy Bay before they started their own estate, and it is the better bottle on your list tonight.",
    opts: ["Say one sentence: there is a Marlborough Sauvignon at forty eight from the pair who used to make Cloudy Bay, you rate it above the Cloudy Bay, and you are happy either way. Then serve whatever he says", "Write down the Cloudy Bay and serve it without comment: he named a good wine and he named it confidently", "Tell him the Dog Point is the better wine and that you would not spend sixty eight pounds on the Cloudy Bay yourself", "Offer to bring a taste of each so the table can decide between them"],
    best: 0,
    why: "You owe him the information once, quietly, to him and not to the table: there is a Marlborough Sauvignon at forty eight made by the viticulturist and a winemaker who were at Cloudy Bay before they founded Dog Point, you rate it higher, and you are happy to pour either. Then you take whatever he says without a second pass. He may still want the famous label in front of his guests, and that is a legitimate reason to spend the extra forty pounds, so the offer has to be neutral and short rather than a correction. Silent compliance is the nearest wrong answer: fast and defensible, but a guest who trusted you never learns the better bottle was there. Option 2 tells him his own choice was foolish and runs down a wine the house stocks. Option 3 fails on mechanics before it fails on manners: tasting both means opening two bottles against one sale, and nothing in the scenario says either is by the glass or under Coravin." },
  { id: "f-half-barolo", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "A couple in their sixties ordered a 2010 Barolo at a hundred and ninety pounds. It is twenty past ten, the plates have gone, and roughly half the bottle is still standing there. She has covered her glass twice. They are asking for coffee.",
    opts: ["Offer to recork and bag the rest for them to take home, assuming your licence permits it, and offer it before they have to ask", "Leave it alone: it is their bottle and their money, and raising it now can read as moving them along", "Offer to pour the remainder out between their glasses so none of it is wasted", "Offer to keep the rest behind the bar under their name for their next visit"],
    best: 0,
    why: "They have paid £190 and are about to walk away from roughly £95 of 2010 Barolo, a strong vintage that is drinking well now and will still be a fine thing at their own table tomorrow. Most guests will not ask, so you offer first, quietly, to the table rather than to her, and you make the offer as you take the coffee order so it reads as service and not as a nudge towards the door. Seal it properly and bag it. If your premises licence does not carry off-sales, or you are in Scotland where off-sales stop at ten and the clock has already beaten you, say so plainly and do not improvise a workaround. The answer worth destroying is not silence, it is pouring the remainder out between their glasses. She has covered her glass twice, which is a no, and filling her glass overrides a guest who has already told you where she stands. Leaving it alone is only a missed opportunity: the couple get home, think about the wine, and remember that nobody mentioned it. Holding the bottle behind the bar is a promise about storage and staff memory that you cannot keep, and an opened Nebbiolo with sixteen years on it is finished within days." },
  { id: "f-price-dispute", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "The bill is on the table. The host, four guests around him, says loudly that you quoted ninety five pounds for the red and the bill says a hundred and forty five. You quoted a hundred and forty five, you are certain of it, and there is no way to prove it. The room is full and the tables either side have stopped talking.",
    opts: ["Charge the ninety five, apologise for the confusion without assigning it, and report the difference to the manager after they have gone", "Bring the list over, show him the price in print, and explain politely that the bill is correct", "Take fifty pounds off as a compromise so that neither of you is out of pocket entirely", "Remove the wine from the bill altogether as a gesture and move on"],
    best: 0,
    why: "You are right, and being right costs more than fifty pounds here: it costs this table and the four that have stopped talking. Take the ninety five, apologise for the confusion rather than for an error, and say it at the volume you would use to describe the wine, not to settle a dispute. The reduction still needs authorisation in the till, so get it away from the table and out of his sight, then give the manager the whole account before service ends so the fifty pounds is recorded as a service decision and not as fifty pounds missing. Comping the bottle is the closer miss: it concedes the same argument, pays ninety five pounds more than the argument was worth, and tells the room the house was in the wrong. Splitting the difference tells him you were never sure of your own price. Producing the list is the outright failure: correct procedure that calls a guest a liar in front of his own guests, which is the one thing he cannot accept and the one thing he will remember." },
  { id: "f-old-chablis", lvl: "advanced",
    s: "Six bottles of 2015 Chablis premier cru Montee de Tonnerre have been on your list since you wrote it. You pull one and taste it at handover: it is honeyed at the edges, still tight in the middle, and it is drinking better than it ever has. It sells perhaps once a fortnight at ninety five pounds. Your general manager has started asking about slow stock.",
    opts: ["Confirm it by tasting, then reposition it as a wine that is ready now, brief the floor on what it tastes like tonight, and sell it hard at ninety five", "Cut the price to seventy five to move all six out before the wine starts to go", "Leave it where it is: it sells now and again, and a wine like that finds its own buyer", "Pull it from the list and use it for staff training and the odd by the glass pour"],
    best: 0,
    why: "Six bottles of an eleven year old Chablis premier cru are not slow stock, they are a wine at its peak with a closing window, and the job is to sell the maturity you paid to keep. Taste it, then hand the floor one accurate sentence: 2015 Montee de Tonnerre, eleven years on, honey and dried citrus at the edge with the Chablis line still running through the middle, drinking now. That sentence is what has been missing, not fifty pounds off the price. The plan has to carry two conditions. First, one bottle is not a verdict on six: brief the team to check each bottle before it reaches the table and to pull any that shows tired, because honeyed notes at this age can be early oxidation as easily as graceful development. Second, set a deadline. 2015 was a warm, ripe vintage in Chablis and matures ahead of the cooler years, so these five go out in weeks, not months. The window is the wine's, the timing of the sale is yours. The discount is the nearest wrong answer: it gives away the margin you earned by holding the wine and tells the room the bottle has a fault, which is untrue. Leaving it alone lets the last bottles fall out of the back of the window. Pulling it for staff training throws away the sale on the one night the wine is finally easy to sell." },
  { id: "f-blank-cheque", lvl: "master",
    s: "A table of eight, a corporate account that books with you most months. The host closes the list, hands it back and says pick something good, we are not counting tonight. They are eating steak, they are loud, and at least half of them are three gin and tonics in. You have a 2015 Clos de la Roche on the list at six hundred and forty pounds.",
    opts: ["Sell two bottles of Saint Joseph at a hundred and ten, tell the host afterwards what you chose and why you saved the Burgundy for a night they will taste it, and mean it", "Sell the Clos de la Roche: he asked for something good, he has the authority to spend, and second guessing him is not your job", "Go back quietly and ask him what sort of number he has in mind, so that nobody is guessing", "Sell one great bottle for the host's end of the table and something sound for the rest"],
    best: 0,
    why: "Protect the bottle, not the invoice. A 2015 Clos de la Roche is grand cru Morey-Saint-Denis from a firm, structured vintage and is still shut at eleven years; poured against seasoned steak in a loud room behind three gin and tonics it gets swallowed rather than drunk, and you cannot buy the bottle back. That is the reason to hold it. But holding it is not a reason to halve the spend. Sell four or five bottles of serious Northern Rhône, a single-vineyard Saint-Joseph or a Cornas around a hundred and fifty, which puts Syrah against the food, lands near the same six hundred pounds for the house, and keeps eight glasses full from first course to coffee. The host said pick something good because he wanted to look generous to his team, so under-pouring him by seventy percent takes from him the thing he was actually buying. And do not explain afterwards what you saved him from, because that is a verdict on his guests: tell him at the door that you have a Clos de la Roche you want him to sit down with properly. That is an invitation and a booking. The nearest wrong answer is asking for a number, which is honest and hands back the one decision he paid you to make in front of his people. Splitting the table builds a visible hierarchy among colleagues who have to work together on Monday." }
];
